WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.420 --> 00:00:03.200
Spiders are amazing.

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:06.540
We’ve learned this mainly from observation.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:09.420
From comparing webs, body shapes,

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:11.469
and seeing the outrageous adaptations

00:00:11.469 --> 00:00:14.020
of spiders in action.

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:16.940
Now, close reads of spider genomes

00:00:16.949 --> 00:00:19.410
are showing that we don’t know the half it.

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:20.410
Looking back through time,

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:23.179
to see how spiders have evolved

00:00:23.179 --> 00:00:25.570
has been especially difficult.

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:29.199
Fossil evidence has shown us early ancestors.

00:00:29.199 --> 00:00:30.779
But the speed of their evolution

00:00:30.779 --> 00:00:33.080
has confounded scientists.

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:35.750
Starting in 2014, two different teams

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:38.370
used genomics to compare 100’s of genes

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:39.980
from 40 spiders.

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:42.070
They found that some orb weavers--

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:45.070
web making spiders--are more genetically similar

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:47.300
to webless spiders than the rest

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:49.080
of the orb-weaving group.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:50.980
This suggests that web-weaving must have

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:54.740
evolved twice, or much earlier in a common ancestor.

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:58.730
A later study comparing 3400 active genes in

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:01.540
70 species indicated that webless,

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:04.540
ground-dwelling spiders, such as jumping spiders

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:08.720
diversified much faster than the web weavers.

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:11.690
The silk genes are where the true complexity of spider genomes

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:13.320
comes into focus.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:15.150
All spiders can make silk.

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:17.560
And they use it for many things:

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:18.380
Webs

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:19.280
Trapping prey

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:20.100
Egg casings

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:21.760
Transport and more

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:25.140
Silk genes have been difficult to characterize

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:27.700
because they code for very large proteins

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:29.820
with highly repetitive regions.

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:34.080
But genome reads of silk glands are showing

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:37.220
there are a lot more silk genes than previously thought.

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:39.960
Take the golden orb weaver genome-

00:01:39.979 --> 00:01:43.020
it codes for 28 silk genes and 8 of them

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:45.540
were unknown before.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:48.229
The genome of cobwebweaving spiders revealed

00:01:48.229 --> 00:01:52.760
a whopping 209 potential silk and glue components.

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:55.760
These findings may help bioengineers harness

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:57.920
silk’s remarkable strength and flexibility

00:01:57.920 --> 00:01:59.300
to make artificial silk.

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:02.720
Understanding spider venom has been daunting

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:04.390
because it can involve a cocktail

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:07.240
of up to 1000 different chemicals and the mixture

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:09.340
varies from one spider to the next.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.279
Scientists have made progress in characterizing

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:14.560
black widow spider venom but getting a

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:17.340
genome sequence has proven difficult.

00:02:17.349 --> 00:02:20.319
Instead they turned to the genome of its relative,

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:22.220
the common house spider.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:25.700
47 toxins known as latrotoxins were found

00:02:25.709 --> 00:02:27.749
in common house spider venom,

00:02:27.749 --> 00:02:29.459
even though these spiders aren’t known

00:02:29.459 --> 00:02:31.340
to have painful bites.

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:34.269
This venom cocktail is different than the black widow’s,

00:02:34.269 --> 00:02:37.950
which includes a toxin called  alpha-latrotoxin,

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:39.849
that does attack vertebrate cells.

00:02:39.849 --> 00:02:41.950
This may have arisen so that black widows

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:45.160
could ensnare prey with back bones like lizards.

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:49.820
Curiously, the house spider’s latrotoxin genes

00:02:49.829 --> 00:02:52.700
match up best with a bacterial gene.

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:55.840
So, researcher’s think a microbe’s gene somehow

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:58.219
got transferred into the ancestor

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:00.880
of both the house and black widow spider.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:04.079
These new spider venom findings will help scientists

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:07.340
refine the search for new drugs and therapies.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:10.680
The complexity of the few spider genomes that have

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:13.860
been sequenced indicate many challenges ahead.

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:17.200
But as this data becomes available to scientists

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:21.160
--the web of spider discoveries will continue to grow.

